%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 190d016defca62041a492cbac019ac5e, type: 3}
  m_Name: PromptsContainer
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PromptsContainer
  DecideNamesHometownsPrefix: "I need you to create some basic profiles for a group
    of fictional college students attending a large public college in Los Angeles.
    For each student, I'll give you their gender, where they're from, and what year
    in college they're in, and you need to decide their names and specific hometowns.
    Names should be appropriate for where they're from. Hometowns can be a major
    city, mid size city, mid size town, suburb, small town, rural locality, anything
    as long as it exists in the real world. \n\nDeliver the output as a json with
    no additional text. The json should have an array of all students called \"students\"
    with five fields for each: (1) gender (exactly as given in the prompt), (2) hometown
    (Format as [City], [Home state or country if international]), (3) year (exactly
    as given in the prompt), (4) first_name (no two students should have the same
    first name), (5) last_name. Here are the profiles:\n\n"
  ExtendedProfilesPrefix: "I'm making a text-based video game where a player plays
    a college student at a large public college in Los Angeles and enters a Survivor-style
    reality competition where each round has a challenge and competitors vote on
    a contestant to eliminate. I need you to generate bios for the some students
    that are NPCs who are also competing in the competition. They should have a lot
    of variety in archetypes and personalities. The information given for each of
    these should vary a lot from person to person, especially in terms of archetypes,
    and be distinct from each other. Make sure they're all well-rounded characters
    who aren't one-note or purely stereotypes.\n\nFor each character, I will give
    you the following information: first name, last name, gender, year, hometown,
    major, some personality or physical traits (you're encouraged to develop them
    beyond these; this is just a starting point), and a zero to ten scale rating
    for their likability (0 is the most unlikable, 10 is the most likable), energy
    (0 is the lowest energy, 10 is the highest energy), and status (0 = thinks of
    themselves as very low status, 10 = thinks of themselves as very high status).
    \n\nBased on this information, for each student, I need you to give me the following
    (each section should be two or more sentences, roughly 200-240 characters in
    length):\n- A physical description (how they look as they show up to the competition;
    body type, current outfit, hair color and style, body language, anything else
    about them that would stand out)\n- A summary of their background (for internal
    use to inform their actions)\n- A summary of their personality (for internal
    use to inform their actions)\n- Perceived personality (what the player, who is
    just meeting them for the first time as the competitors arrive before the competition
    starts, might observe, or how they appear based on first impressions. Avoid talking
    about what others think about them or telling the player how to feel about them,
    focusing only on observable details like \"they smile a lot\" or \"they seem
    nervous\" or \"their posture is very rigid\" or \"they're being very social and
    seem nice\")\n\nDeliver the output as a json with no additional text. The json
    should have an array of all students called \"students\" with the following fields
    (all as strings except the energy/likability/status values, which are ints):
    first_name, last_name, gender, year, hometown, major, likability, energy, status,
    physical_description, background, personality, perceived_personality.\n\nHere
    are the profiles:\n\n"
  ProfessorGenPrefix: 'I need you to generate a profile for a college professor or
    course instructor who has volunteered to create and judge an original challenge
    for a competition for students at the college they teach at. The challenge can
    be of any nature, with subjective or objective results, but there will be # students
    competing (all as individuals; no teams) and 1 winner of the challenge. Assume
    there will be no ties. Make sure they''re a well-rounded character who isn''t
    one-note or purely stereotypes.


    I need the following information about
    the professor/instructor:

    - Full name

    - Name students should use when
    addressing them (Professor X, Dr. X, Mr./Ms./Mrs./Mx. X, just their first name,
    Coach X, whatever they prefer)

    - Hometown (formatted as [city name], [state
    or country if international])

    - Physical description ( (how they look as
    they show up to the competition; body type, current outfit, hair color and style,
    body language, anything else about them that would stand out)

    - A summary
    of their background (for internal use to inform their actions)

    - A summary
    of their personality (for internal use to inform their actions)

    - Perceived
    personality (what the competitors, who are just meeting them for the first time,
    might observe, or how they appear based on first impressions. Avoid talking about
    what others think about them or telling the reader how to feel about them, focusing
    only on observable details like "they smile a lot" or "they seem nervous" or
    "their posture is very rigid" or "they''re being very social and seem nice")

    -
    The announcement of their challenge. Write this in first person as the professor/instructor,
    verbatim as they would explain the challenge in full to the competing students.
    This announcement should contain all information necessary for someone to write
    out exactly how they did the entire challenge (for example, no surprises, no
    missing/external information outside of this announcement, no inclusion of documents
    students would have to read, objects or images that must be studied, etc. there
    should be no need to gather any information outside of what is present in the
    announcement). Once the announcement is given, the challenge will start. No questions
    or follow-up. All participants should be given the same materials and information
    to complete the task. Do not explicitly mention the number of participants, winners,
    or lack of ties or teams, since this is already implied. Challenges should never
    be of a nature where there is a single, correct answer. Assume all participants
    always have access to the complete text of the announcement itself (for example,
    no memory exercises relating to the announcement). Since this is part of a broadcasted
    competition, the process of completing the challenge should be at least somewhat
    entertaining to an audience. All challenges should be completable by any college
    student regardless of field of study, and should not require any specialized
    knowledge.

    - A short summary of the task for that challenge. A one sentence
    answer to the question, "what did the competitors have to do in this challenge?"


    Deliver
    the output as a json with no additional text. The json should have the following
    arguments: full_name, referred_name, gender, hometown, department, physical_description,
    background, personality, challenge_announcement, challenge_summary.


    Here''s
    some information about them to get you started:


'
  ExistingProfs: '


    The following challenges and challenge-givers already exist,
    so make sure the challenge you generate here is sufficiently different from these,
    with extra emphasis on challenges that reward different skills (for example,
    athleticism, intelligence, creativity, strategy, performance, charisma, endurance,
    tolerance of something uncomfortable, speed, etc.):


'
  FirstImpressions: '$f is a college $y who is arriving at a reality-style competition
    at their college with rounds of challenges and eliminations. Here''s $f''s bio:


    $b


    And
    here is how $f is trying to come off when meeting the other competitors for the
    first time:


    $p


    Keep in mind that this is merely how they''re attempting
    to present themselves, and the actual result may be different. If they try to
    do something that might not work (for example, pretend they''re someone they''re
    not, use a skill or knowledge it doesn''t look like they have, try to outdo other
    competitors present, etc.), feel free to make them fail. Use their bio to judge
    how much their presentation of themselves does or doesn''t go according to plan.


    Based
    on this, summarize in around 200-240 characters what other competitors, who are
    just meeting $f for the first time, might observe about $f or how $f might appear
    based on first impressions. Avoid talking about what others think or feel about
    them, focusing only on observable details like "$f smiles a lot" or "$f seems
    nervous" or "$f''s posture is very rigid" or "$f is being very social and seems
    nice."'
  PlayerChallengeAttempt: '$f is a college $y who is competing in a reality-style
    competition at their college with various challenges. I want you to review the
    following challenge, $f''s bio, and a description of what $f is trying to do,
    and, based on what you know about $po, decide to what degree they succeed or
    fail in the execution of what $ps is trying to do, and, based on that, summarize
    $pp execution of the challenge. Do not explicitly say "$ps succeeded in $pp attempt"
    or "$ps failed in $pp attempt." Only describe the final product/execution of
    the challenge so that it may be judged, leaving out anything a judge wouldn''t
    be able to see like his thoughts or internal plans.


    Challenge description
    (as given by the giver of the challenge):


    $c


    $f''s bio:


    $b


    What
    $f is attempting to do:


    $a'
  GiveNpcChallenge: 'It''s time for a challenge!


    This challenge is brought
    to you by $tf, who teaches $d.


    $pd

    $pp


    $n announces the challenge:


    $a


    Describe
    $f''s execution of the challenge. Use their bio to inform any creative choices
    they may make, how hard they try to succeed, and the degree to which $f succeeds
    or fails.  If the challenge involves writing or creating something, don''t create
    it all here, but give a summary of what $f writes/creates and the quality of
    the execution. Describe ONLY their execution, not their thoughts, process, how
    well or badly it went according to plan, or $n''s thoughts on the final product.
    This should only describe a summary of what $n sees as the final result and nothing
    else.'
  GetChallengeJudgment: '$fn is a fictional character who teaches $d at a large public
    college in Los Angeles. There''s a competition at the college where $n teaches,
    involving students competing in various challenges. $n created and is the judge
    of one of the challenges in this competition. Here is a description of the challenge
    exactly as $n announced it:


    $a


    Here''s a summary of the execution
    of each competitor''s execution of the challenge:


    $s


    I need you to
    have $n review each student''s result of the challenge individually, give each
    contestant feedback on how they did in the challenge, and decide on exactly one
    student to be declared as the winner of this challenge, and announce that winner.
    Use the following bio to inform $n''s decisions:


    $b


    Deliver the output
    as a json with no additional text. The json should have the following arguments:

    -
    feedback (an array of objects, each with two fields: contestant_name and review)

    -
    winner_announcement

    - winner_name'
  InitiateConversation: '$r


    $n is now free to have a private conversation with
    one other competitor of $pp choice, or $ps can skip that conversation. $n can
    talk about anything, be that forming an alliance, confronting an enemy, congratulating
    someone who did well, discussing who to vote for in the upcoming elimination,
    or any other reason.


    The available people to talk to are: $c


    Tell
    me who $n chooses to talk to and what $n says to start the conversation. Present
    this as a json with no other text, with two arguments: name (exactly as stated
    in this prompt, or "Nobody" if $n doesn''t want to initiate a conversation with
    anyone) and opening_line (leave as empty string if $n chooses to talk to nobody).'
  ConversationInstructionsInitiate: '$nl comes up to $ns.


'
  ConversationInstructions: '$nl says to $ns: "$d"


    Tell me what $ns says in
    response, and whether or not $ns continues the conversation after $ns says that
    line of dialogue.


    Ouput the result as a json file with no other text, containing
    the following arguments:

    - dialogue (what $ns says in response)

    - conversation_status
    (either "continue" if $ns lets the conversation continue, or "end" if this is
    the end of the conversation)'
  ChooseWhoToEliminate: "It's time for $n to choose someone to eliminate from the
    competition. Up for elimination are the following competitors: $e. $w is safe
    from elimination for winning the challenge.\n\n$n needs to pick one competitor
    to vote for to be eliminated. The competitor who receives the most votes will
    be removed from the competition permanently. All votes will be cast publicly,
    so everyone will know who everyone else voted for. \n\nKeep in mind that $n can
    choose who to vote off for a variety of reasons; $ps can vote for the person
    who's doing the worst job, whoever $ps thinks will receive the most votes from
    the other competitors, someone $ps thinks is a threat (either because they're
    likely to win or because they might vote $n out), whoever $ps likes the least,
    or any other strategic reasons.\n\nWhich of the following people does $n vote
    to eliminate from the competition (only include the name, exactly as shown in
    this list, with no other text)?:\n\n$o"
  ChooseOtherFinalist: '$w has earned a spot in the final two! And with it, the power
    to choose who the other person in the final will be.


    The two options are
    $o. One will go to the final, and the other will be eliminated right now. The
    final round is not a challenge like the others. The two finalists will both get
    a chance to make their case for why they should win, and then all eliminated
    contestants will get a chance to vote for who should win the entire competition.
    Whoever receives the most votes gets the crown.


    Which one does $w choose
    to advance to the final? $w could choose whichever person they like more, or
    they could choose the person they think they''d have the best chance of defeating
    to win the whole thing.


    $o?'
  NpcFinalistSpeech: "$n has made it to the final round! Now all $n has to do is
    win a vote conducted by all the eliminated contestants. Whoever gets the most
    votes will be declared the winner. \n\n$n has a chance to make their case to
    the eliminated players. So, when asked, \"why should you be declared the winner
    of this competition and not $o?\" what does $n say?"
  ChooseWinnerVote: 'It''s the final round, and it''s time to choose the winner of
    the competition: either $o.


    $n may be out of the running, but $n and the
    other eliminated contestants each get to vote for who they think the winner should
    be. Whoever gets the most votes will be crowned winner of the entire competition.


    When
    asked to make their case about why they should win, they said the following:


    $s


    $n
    will get a chance to tell each finalist their thoughts on the game that finalist
    played throughout, then to announce the finalist that gets their vote.


    Output
    the result as a json file with no other text, containing the following arguments:

    -
    name ($n)

    - messages (an array of objects representing $n telling each finalist
    what they thought about their performance overall, with these arguments: name
    (name of who they''re addressing, exactly as stated in this prompt), message
    (what $n says to them)

    - vote_announcement ($n announcing who got their
    vote and why)

    - vote (the name of the finalist $n voted for, exactly as
    it appears in this prompt)'
